1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,161
On many airlines,

2
00:00:01,161 --> 00:00:03,689
the captain and the first officer are

3
00:00:03,689 --> 00:00:05,944
not allowed to eat the same meal.

4
00:00:06,124 --> 00:00:07,534
Not because of taste,

5
00:00:07,534 --> 00:00:09,953
because of what happened on a flight

6
00:00:09,953 --> 00:00:11,632
in nineteen seventy-five,

7
00:00:11,632 --> 00:00:13,983
when a breakfast made more than one

8
00:00:13,983 --> 00:00:16,468
hundred forty people sick in the air.

9
00:00:16,648 --> 00:00:19,412
The cockpit is full of rules and

10
00:00:19,412 --> 00:00:20,967
objects like that.

11
00:00:21,147 --> 00:00:21,937
An axe.

12
00:00:22,117 --> 00:00:23,707
A rope above the window.

13
00:00:23,887 --> 00:00:26,616
Switches hidden under red covers.

14
00:00:26,796 --> 00:00:30,837
Today, ten cockpit details pilots know and

15
00:00:30,837 --> 00:00:33,147
passengers never notice,

16
00:00:33,147 --> 00:00:35,457
and why each one exists.

17
00:00:35,806 --> 00:00:38,105
The cockpit of an airliner is the

18
00:00:38,105 --> 00:00:41,170
most carefully designed office in the world.

19
00:00:41,350 --> 00:00:43,560
Every switch has a position,

20
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:45,375
every item has a place,

21
00:00:45,375 --> 00:00:48,058
and many of the rules were written

22
00:00:48,058 --> 00:00:50,661
after an accident or a near miss.

23
00:00:50,841 --> 00:00:52,364
From the outside,

24
00:00:52,364 --> 00:00:55,139
the pilots seem to just sit and

25
00:00:55,139 --> 00:00:55,497
fly.

26
00:00:55,677 --> 00:00:58,975
Inside, almost everything they do and use

27
00:00:58,975 --> 00:01:01,388
has a reason that goes back to

28
00:01:01,388 --> 00:01:02,434
a real event.

29
00:01:02,784 --> 00:01:05,820
As always, every fact here comes from

30
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:07,051
a named source,

31
00:01:07,051 --> 00:01:10,496
the Federal Aviation Administration rules,

32
00:01:10,496 --> 00:01:13,942
official reports, and aviation references,

33
00:01:13,942 --> 00:01:16,403
all listed in the description.

34
00:01:16,583 --> 00:01:18,057
Let's start with lunch.

35
00:01:18,407 --> 00:01:21,218
Why do pilots on many airlines eat

36
00:01:21,218 --> 00:01:22,541
different meals?

37
00:01:22,721 --> 00:01:26,009
Because if both pilots get food poisoning

38
00:01:26,009 --> 00:01:27,372
at the same time,

39
00:01:27,372 --> 00:01:29,859
there is nobody left to fly the

40
00:01:29,859 --> 00:01:30,581
airplane.

41
00:01:30,761 --> 00:01:32,265
On February third,

42
00:01:32,265 --> 00:01:36,194
nineteen seventy-five, a Japan Air Lines flight

43
00:01:36,194 --> 00:01:39,036
stopped in Anchorage on its way to

44
00:01:39,036 --> 00:01:39,956
Copenhagen.

45
00:01:40,136 --> 00:01:42,517
The breakfast was ham omelettes,

46
00:01:42,517 --> 00:01:45,568
contaminated by a cook with infected cuts

47
00:01:45,568 --> 00:01:46,684
on his fingers.

48
00:01:46,864 --> 00:01:48,318
About an hour later,

49
00:01:48,318 --> 00:01:51,446
one hundred forty-four people on board were

50
00:01:51,446 --> 00:01:51,810
sick.

51
00:01:52,159 --> 00:01:54,899
The flight landed safely for one reason.

52
00:01:55,079 --> 00:01:56,231
By pure chance,

53
00:01:56,231 --> 00:01:59,225
the captain and first officer had eaten

54
00:01:59,225 --> 00:02:01,604
steak instead of the omelettes.

55
00:02:01,784 --> 00:02:04,498
The airline had no rule about crew

56
00:02:04,498 --> 00:02:04,977
meals.

57
00:02:05,157 --> 00:02:09,191
An investigator recommended that cockpit crews eat

58
00:02:09,191 --> 00:02:11,935
meals prepared by different cooks,

59
00:02:11,935 --> 00:02:14,758
and many airlines adopted the idea.

60
00:02:14,938 --> 00:02:17,333
Today, it is common for the captain

61
00:02:17,333 --> 00:02:20,207
and first officer to get different dishes,

62
00:02:20,207 --> 00:02:22,465
and some airlines ask them not to

63
00:02:22,465 --> 00:02:23,901
eat at the same time.

64
00:02:24,252 --> 00:02:27,801
There is no Federal Aviation Administration rule

65
00:02:27,801 --> 00:02:29,058
that forces this.

66
00:02:29,238 --> 00:02:30,757
It is a company policy,

67
00:02:30,757 --> 00:02:33,464
and different airlines do it differently.

68
00:02:33,644 --> 00:02:36,118
But the logic is simple and very

69
00:02:36,118 --> 00:02:36,814
aviation.

70
00:02:36,994 --> 00:02:40,091
Never let one single problem knock out

71
00:02:40,091 --> 00:02:41,069
both pilots.

72
00:02:41,249 --> 00:02:43,724
You will see that idea again and

73
00:02:43,724 --> 00:02:45,271
again in this video,

74
00:02:45,271 --> 00:02:47,669
in places you would not expect.

75
00:02:48,019 --> 00:02:50,534
Why does the captain of an airliner

76
00:02:50,534 --> 00:02:52,546
always sit in the left seat?

77
00:02:52,726 --> 00:02:55,230
There is no single law of physics

78
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:55,989
behind it.

79
00:02:56,169 --> 00:02:59,153
One common explanation goes back to early

80
00:02:59,153 --> 00:03:02,647
aviation, when pilots usually turned left around

81
00:03:02,647 --> 00:03:05,121
airfields and in holding patterns,

82
00:03:05,121 --> 00:03:07,523
and sitting on the left gave them

83
00:03:07,523 --> 00:03:09,416
the best view in the turn.

84
00:03:09,596 --> 00:03:12,149
Over time, the left seat became the

85
00:03:12,149 --> 00:03:15,650
command seat, and airliners were designed around

86
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:18,787
it, with the controls and displays arranged

87
00:03:18,787 --> 00:03:20,683
for a captain on the left.

88
00:03:21,034 --> 00:03:22,496
On many airliners,

89
00:03:22,496 --> 00:03:25,260
only the left seat has the tiller,

90
00:03:25,260 --> 00:03:27,779
a small wheel used to steer the

91
00:03:27,779 --> 00:03:29,811
nose wheel while taxiing.

92
00:03:29,991 --> 00:03:31,427
So on the ground,

93
00:03:31,427 --> 00:03:34,553
the captain often taxis the airplane.

94
00:03:34,733 --> 00:03:35,714
In the air,

95
00:03:35,714 --> 00:03:38,657
the two pilots take turns flying,

96
00:03:38,657 --> 00:03:41,599
one as pilot flying and the other

97
00:03:41,599 --> 00:03:43,383
as pilot monitoring.

98
00:03:43,563 --> 00:03:46,502
The four stripes on the captain's sleeve

99
00:03:46,502 --> 00:03:48,780
and the left seat both mean the

100
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:49,588
same thing.

101
00:03:49,768 --> 00:03:52,044
The final decision is made there.

102
00:03:52,394 --> 00:03:55,203
Helicopters are often the opposite.

103
00:03:55,383 --> 00:03:57,025
In many helicopters,

104
00:03:57,025 --> 00:03:59,651
the pilot in command sits on the

105
00:03:59,651 --> 00:04:00,143
right.

106
00:04:00,323 --> 00:04:03,256
The usual explanation is that the right

107
00:04:03,256 --> 00:04:05,137
hand stays on the cyclic,

108
00:04:05,137 --> 00:04:07,167
the most important control,

109
00:04:07,167 --> 00:04:09,800
while the left hand can briefly let

110
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:12,282
go of the collective to reach the

111
00:04:12,282 --> 00:04:14,011
switches in the center.

112
00:04:14,191 --> 00:04:16,978
The seat is not about tradition alone.

113
00:04:17,158 --> 00:04:19,406
It is about where the hands need

114
00:04:19,406 --> 00:04:19,828
to be.

115
00:04:20,178 --> 00:04:23,100
Why do pilots stop chatting below ten

116
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,206
thousand feet?

117
00:04:24,386 --> 00:04:26,836
Because of the sterile cockpit rule.

118
00:04:27,016 --> 00:04:29,139
During takeoff, landing,

119
00:04:29,139 --> 00:04:32,502
taxi and any flight below ten thousand

120
00:04:32,502 --> 00:04:34,183
feet except cruise,

121
00:04:34,183 --> 00:04:38,696
pilots are forbidden from non-essential activities.

122
00:04:38,876 --> 00:04:40,931
No casual conversation,

123
00:04:40,931 --> 00:04:44,596
no eating, no reading unrelated material,

124
00:04:44,596 --> 00:04:48,082
and no non-urgent calls from the cabin.

125
00:04:48,262 --> 00:04:51,192
The rule was introduced by the Federal

126
00:04:51,192 --> 00:04:54,817
Aviation Administration in nineteen eighty-one,

127
00:04:54,817 --> 00:04:57,670
after a series of accidents caused by

128
00:04:57,670 --> 00:04:58,595
distraction.

129
00:04:58,945 --> 00:05:01,740
One of those accidents was Eastern Air

130
00:05:01,740 --> 00:05:05,343
Lines Flight two twelve in nineteen seventy-four.

131
00:05:05,523 --> 00:05:08,184
It crashed short of the runway at

132
00:05:08,184 --> 00:05:10,039
Charlotte in dense fog.

133
00:05:10,219 --> 00:05:13,087
Investigators found that the crew had been

134
00:05:13,087 --> 00:05:16,569
talking about unrelated topics during the approach,

135
00:05:16,569 --> 00:05:18,822
and lost track of their altitude.

136
00:05:19,002 --> 00:05:21,120
The idea of the rule is that

137
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:23,996
the most dangerous minutes of a flight

138
00:05:23,996 --> 00:05:27,173
deserve one hundred percent of the pilots'

139
00:05:27,173 --> 00:05:27,930
attention.

140
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:31,046
That is also why flight attendants are

141
00:05:31,046 --> 00:05:34,031
careful about when they call the cockpit,

142
00:05:34,031 --> 00:05:36,070
and why you may hear a chime

143
00:05:36,070 --> 00:05:38,909
pattern instead of a normal call during

144
00:05:38,909 --> 00:05:40,365
takeoff and landing.

145
00:05:40,545 --> 00:05:42,755
The cabin and the cockpit have a

146
00:05:42,755 --> 00:05:45,517
shared code for what is important enough

147
00:05:45,517 --> 00:05:47,175
to interrupt the pilots.

148
00:05:47,355 --> 00:05:49,140
In the critical minutes,

149
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:51,000
only safety gets through.

150
00:05:51,351 --> 00:05:54,374
Why do pilots have special oxygen masks

151
00:05:54,374 --> 00:05:55,925
next to their seats?

152
00:05:56,105 --> 00:05:59,046
Because if the cabin loses pressure at

153
00:05:59,046 --> 00:06:02,065
high altitude, a person can lose useful

154
00:06:02,065 --> 00:06:04,000
consciousness in seconds.

155
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:06,838
So the rules say the pilots' mask

156
00:06:06,838 --> 00:06:09,335
must be placed on the face from

157
00:06:09,335 --> 00:06:10,865
its ready position,

158
00:06:10,865 --> 00:06:13,121
sealed and supplying oxygen,

159
00:06:13,121 --> 00:06:14,248
with one hand,

160
00:06:14,248 --> 00:06:15,859
within five seconds.

161
00:06:16,039 --> 00:06:18,129
It is a quick donning mask.

162
00:06:18,309 --> 00:06:20,012
You squeeze the harness,

163
00:06:20,012 --> 00:06:22,423
it inflates, you pull it over your

164
00:06:22,423 --> 00:06:24,764
head, and it tightens on its own.

165
00:06:25,115 --> 00:06:27,562
The masks are tested before flight,

166
00:06:27,562 --> 00:06:30,219
and pilots practice putting them on in

167
00:06:30,219 --> 00:06:31,198
the simulator.

168
00:06:31,378 --> 00:06:33,946
Many also include smoke goggles,

169
00:06:33,946 --> 00:06:37,477
because the same mask protects against smoke

170
00:06:37,477 --> 00:06:38,681
in the cockpit.

171
00:06:38,861 --> 00:06:40,907
And at very high altitude,

172
00:06:40,907 --> 00:06:43,110
the rules are even stricter.

173
00:06:43,290 --> 00:06:44,668
Under US rules,

174
00:06:44,668 --> 00:06:47,425
above forty-one thousand feet,

175
00:06:47,425 --> 00:06:50,640
one pilot at the controls must wear

176
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:53,397
and use the oxygen mask at all

177
00:06:53,397 --> 00:06:53,948
times.

178
00:06:54,128 --> 00:06:56,142
Two pilots, two masks,

179
00:06:56,142 --> 00:06:58,064
and one always ready.

180
00:06:58,414 --> 00:07:00,662
Passengers have their own masks,

181
00:07:00,662 --> 00:07:02,489
but they work differently.

182
00:07:02,669 --> 00:07:05,105
The yellow masks in the cabin are

183
00:07:05,105 --> 00:07:08,279
fed by chemical oxygen generators that last

184
00:07:08,279 --> 00:07:09,681
only a few minutes,

185
00:07:09,681 --> 00:07:12,265
enough for the pilots to descend to

186
00:07:12,265 --> 00:07:15,660
an altitude where people can breathe normally.

187
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:19,285
The pilots' masks are connected to oxygen

188
00:07:19,285 --> 00:07:22,058
bottles and can last much longer,

189
00:07:22,058 --> 00:07:25,250
because the pilots must stay sharp for

190
00:07:25,250 --> 00:07:28,611
the whole emergency descent and landing.

191
00:07:28,961 --> 00:07:31,220
Why is there a rope stored above

192
00:07:31,220 --> 00:07:34,256
the cockpit side windows on many airliners?

193
00:07:34,436 --> 00:07:37,268
Because those side windows can open.

194
00:07:37,448 --> 00:07:39,798
On jets like the Airbus A three

195
00:07:39,798 --> 00:07:42,072
twenty and many Boeing models,

196
00:07:42,072 --> 00:07:44,194
the side windows slide back.

197
00:07:44,374 --> 00:07:47,019
If the cockpit door is blocked after

198
00:07:47,019 --> 00:07:49,664
an accident, the pilots can open the

199
00:07:49,664 --> 00:07:51,868
window, drop the rope outside,

200
00:07:51,868 --> 00:07:53,999
and climb down to the ground.

201
00:07:54,179 --> 00:07:57,162
It is the pilots' own emergency exit.

202
00:07:57,513 --> 00:08:00,775
The windows are also useful in normal

203
00:08:00,775 --> 00:08:01,216
life.

204
00:08:01,396 --> 00:08:04,180
Pilots can open them on the ground

205
00:08:04,180 --> 00:08:06,063
to talk to ground crew,

206
00:08:06,063 --> 00:08:07,291
to clear smoke,

207
00:08:07,291 --> 00:08:09,256
or simply to see better.

208
00:08:09,436 --> 00:08:10,588
In some cases,

209
00:08:10,588 --> 00:08:13,713
pilots have even flown with the window

210
00:08:13,713 --> 00:08:16,016
open at low speed to see the

211
00:08:16,016 --> 00:08:19,142
runway after a windshield was damaged.

212
00:08:19,322 --> 00:08:21,890
A window that opens on an airplane

213
00:08:21,890 --> 00:08:25,440
flying at forty thousand feet sounds dangerous.

214
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:26,605
On the ground,

215
00:08:26,605 --> 00:08:28,151
it can save your life.

216
00:08:28,501 --> 00:08:31,056
The rope is not the only backup

217
00:08:31,056 --> 00:08:31,880
exit idea.

218
00:08:32,060 --> 00:08:33,880
On some larger airplanes,

219
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:37,520
such as certain Boeing seven forty-seven versions,

220
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:40,068
the flight deck has an escape hatch

221
00:08:40,068 --> 00:08:42,689
in the ceiling with descent devices,

222
00:08:42,689 --> 00:08:45,529
because the cockpit sits high above the

223
00:08:45,529 --> 00:08:46,038
ground.

224
00:08:46,218 --> 00:08:47,819
Whatever the design,

225
00:08:47,819 --> 00:08:49,899
the principle is the same.

226
00:08:50,079 --> 00:08:52,186
The pilots must always have a way

227
00:08:52,186 --> 00:08:54,165
out that does not depend on the

228
00:08:54,165 --> 00:08:54,549
cabin.

229
00:08:54,899 --> 00:08:56,673
Why is there an axe in the

230
00:08:56,673 --> 00:08:58,242
cockpit of an airliner?

231
00:08:58,422 --> 00:08:59,631
Under US rules,

232
00:08:59,631 --> 00:09:03,581
airplanes with more than nineteen passenger seats

233
00:09:03,581 --> 00:09:05,435
must carry a crash axe.

234
00:09:05,615 --> 00:09:06,892
It is not a weapon.

235
00:09:07,072 --> 00:09:09,115
It is a tool for fire.

236
00:09:09,295 --> 00:09:11,706
A fire can start behind a wall

237
00:09:11,706 --> 00:09:14,438
panel, in wiring you cannot reach.

238
00:09:14,618 --> 00:09:16,897
The axe lets the crew cut through

239
00:09:16,897 --> 00:09:18,968
the panel to find the fire and

240
00:09:18,968 --> 00:09:21,108
attack it with an extinguisher.

241
00:09:21,288 --> 00:09:23,674
It can also be used to remove

242
00:09:23,674 --> 00:09:27,128
barriers or cabin parts after an accident.

243
00:09:27,478 --> 00:09:29,922
The crash axe is kept where the

244
00:09:29,922 --> 00:09:31,341
crew can reach it,

245
00:09:31,341 --> 00:09:34,731
but passengers cannot during normal flight,

246
00:09:34,731 --> 00:09:36,545
usually in the cockpit.

247
00:09:36,725 --> 00:09:39,264
Its handle is typically insulated,

248
00:09:39,264 --> 00:09:42,102
because the panels it cuts through are

249
00:09:42,102 --> 00:09:43,970
full of electrical wires.

250
00:09:44,150 --> 00:09:45,984
A hidden fire is one of the

251
00:09:45,984 --> 00:09:48,497
most dangerous things on an airplane,

252
00:09:48,497 --> 00:09:50,806
because by the time you see smoke,

253
00:09:50,806 --> 00:09:52,844
it may have been growing for a

254
00:09:52,844 --> 00:09:53,252
while.

255
00:09:53,432 --> 00:09:56,114
The axe exists to buy time.

256
00:09:56,463 --> 00:09:59,203
The cockpit has other tools for fire.

257
00:09:59,383 --> 00:10:02,344
There is a halon fire extinguisher,

258
00:10:02,344 --> 00:10:06,659
protective breathing equipment that covers the head

259
00:10:06,659 --> 00:10:08,351
and supplies oxygen,

260
00:10:08,351 --> 00:10:12,073
and heat resistant gloves on many airplanes.

261
00:10:12,253 --> 00:10:14,879
The crew trains to fight fires in

262
00:10:14,879 --> 00:10:17,187
the cabin and in the cockpit.

263
00:10:17,367 --> 00:10:18,430
On an airplane,

264
00:10:18,430 --> 00:10:20,768
you cannot pull over and wait for

265
00:10:20,768 --> 00:10:22,186
the fire department.

266
00:10:22,366 --> 00:10:24,676
The crew is the fire department.

267
00:10:25,025 --> 00:10:27,580
Why is the windshield of an airliner

268
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:29,495
heated, even on a warm day?

269
00:10:29,675 --> 00:10:30,952
Two reasons.

270
00:10:31,132 --> 00:10:33,718
First, to stop ice and fog from

271
00:10:33,718 --> 00:10:35,219
blocking the view.

272
00:10:35,399 --> 00:10:37,451
Second, and less obvious,

273
00:10:37,451 --> 00:10:40,652
heat makes the layers of the windshield

274
00:10:40,652 --> 00:10:41,309
tougher.

275
00:10:41,489 --> 00:10:45,229
An airliner windshield is a sandwich of

276
00:10:45,229 --> 00:10:47,627
glass and plastic layers,

277
00:10:47,627 --> 00:10:51,848
and warm plastic absorbs impacts better than

278
00:10:51,848 --> 00:10:53,958
cold, brittle plastic.

279
00:10:54,138 --> 00:10:55,624
And it needs to.

280
00:10:55,804 --> 00:10:58,857
The rules say a windshield must survive

281
00:10:58,857 --> 00:11:01,363
hitting a four pound bird at the

282
00:11:01,363 --> 00:11:04,964
airplane's design cruising speed at sea level.

283
00:11:05,314 --> 00:11:07,363
The tail is held to an even

284
00:11:07,363 --> 00:11:08,577
higher standard.

285
00:11:08,757 --> 00:11:11,515
It must survive an eight pound bird,

286
00:11:11,515 --> 00:11:14,349
because a damaged tail could make the

287
00:11:14,349 --> 00:11:16,187
airplane uncontrollable.

288
00:11:16,367 --> 00:11:17,993
Birds are not a theory.

289
00:11:18,173 --> 00:11:21,341
Most bird strikes happen near the ground,

290
00:11:21,341 --> 00:11:23,427
during takeoff and landing,

291
00:11:23,427 --> 00:11:26,054
which is exactly when a pilot most

292
00:11:26,054 --> 00:11:27,368
needs to see out.

293
00:11:27,548 --> 00:11:30,477
A windshield that cracks but holds is

294
00:11:30,477 --> 00:11:32,378
a successful windshield.

295
00:11:32,727 --> 00:11:35,793
You may also notice that cockpit windshields

296
00:11:35,793 --> 00:11:38,300
have wipers, which surprises people.

297
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:39,604
At high speed,

298
00:11:39,604 --> 00:11:42,494
the airflow often clears the rain on

299
00:11:42,494 --> 00:11:43,136
its own.

300
00:11:43,316 --> 00:11:44,933
But at slow speed,

301
00:11:44,933 --> 00:11:47,899
during taxi, takeoff and landing,

302
00:11:47,899 --> 00:11:50,235
rain can sit on the glass.

303
00:11:50,415 --> 00:11:52,773
So the wipers are used exactly in

304
00:11:52,773 --> 00:11:55,559
the moments when the view matters most.

305
00:11:55,739 --> 00:11:59,586
Some airliners also use special coatings that

306
00:11:59,586 --> 00:12:02,066
make water bead and run away.

307
00:12:02,416 --> 00:12:06,267
Why are some cockpit switches hidden under

308
00:12:06,267 --> 00:12:09,475
small covers, often red or striped?

309
00:12:09,655 --> 00:12:12,853
Because some switches must never be moved

310
00:12:12,853 --> 00:12:13,788
by accident.

311
00:12:13,968 --> 00:12:16,529
Things like engine fuel cutoff,

312
00:12:16,529 --> 00:12:19,833
emergency power, or a system that cannot

313
00:12:19,833 --> 00:12:21,898
easily be turned back on.

314
00:12:22,078 --> 00:12:24,716
The cover forces the pilot to do

315
00:12:24,716 --> 00:12:26,448
two separate actions,

316
00:12:26,448 --> 00:12:27,684
lift the guard,

317
00:12:27,684 --> 00:12:29,416
then move the switch.

318
00:12:29,596 --> 00:12:32,749
That extra step turns an accidental bump

319
00:12:32,749 --> 00:12:35,981
into something that almost cannot happen.

320
00:12:36,331 --> 00:12:39,965
Cockpit designers use many tricks like this.

321
00:12:40,145 --> 00:12:42,828
Some switches must be pulled out before

322
00:12:42,828 --> 00:12:43,791
they can move.

323
00:12:43,971 --> 00:12:47,085
Some levers have different shapes so they

324
00:12:47,085 --> 00:12:49,136
can be identified by touch,

325
00:12:49,136 --> 00:12:51,111
even in smoke or darkness.

326
00:12:51,291 --> 00:12:54,269
The landing gear lever on many airplanes

327
00:12:54,269 --> 00:12:56,353
ends in a small wheel shape,

328
00:12:56,353 --> 00:12:58,437
and the flap lever in a wing

329
00:12:58,437 --> 00:12:58,884
shape.

330
00:12:59,064 --> 00:13:01,440
A pilot's hand can tell them apart

331
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:02,559
without looking.

332
00:13:02,908 --> 00:13:05,648
Color is part of the language too.

333
00:13:05,828 --> 00:13:07,395
In modern cockpits,

334
00:13:07,395 --> 00:13:10,527
red usually means a warning that needs

335
00:13:10,527 --> 00:13:13,907
immediate action, amber or yellow means a

336
00:13:13,907 --> 00:13:17,287
caution, and green or white means normal.

337
00:13:17,467 --> 00:13:20,292
Many airliners are designed with a dark

338
00:13:20,292 --> 00:13:23,551
cockpit philosophy, where lights are off when

339
00:13:23,551 --> 00:13:25,072
everything is normal.

340
00:13:25,252 --> 00:13:26,901
If a light comes on,

341
00:13:26,901 --> 00:13:29,292
something deserves attention.

342
00:13:29,472 --> 00:13:32,967
Silence and darkness mean all is well.

343
00:13:33,316 --> 00:13:35,352
Why are there big handles in the

344
00:13:35,352 --> 00:13:37,643
middle of the cockpit that can light

345
00:13:37,643 --> 00:13:38,088
up red?

346
00:13:38,268 --> 00:13:40,660
They are the engine fire handles.

347
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:42,916
If an engine catches fire,

348
00:13:42,916 --> 00:13:45,552
its handle lights up and an alarm

349
00:13:45,552 --> 00:13:46,111
sounds.

350
00:13:46,291 --> 00:13:47,768
On many airliners,

351
00:13:47,768 --> 00:13:50,559
pulling the handle shuts off fuel,

352
00:13:50,559 --> 00:13:53,596
hydraulic fluid and bleed air to that

353
00:13:53,596 --> 00:13:56,798
engine and arms the fire extinguishers.

354
00:13:56,978 --> 00:14:00,034
Turning it then fires an extinguisher bottle

355
00:14:00,034 --> 00:14:01,146
into the engine.

356
00:14:01,326 --> 00:14:03,636
If the fire is still burning,

357
00:14:03,636 --> 00:14:06,504
the pilots can fire a second bottle.

358
00:14:06,853 --> 00:14:09,021
Before pulling a fire handle,

359
00:14:09,021 --> 00:14:12,309
pilots confirm which engine they are dealing

360
00:14:12,309 --> 00:14:15,224
with, often by both touching the handle

361
00:14:15,224 --> 00:14:16,943
and saying it out loud.

362
00:14:17,123 --> 00:14:19,851
Shutting down the wrong engine is one

363
00:14:19,851 --> 00:14:22,358
of the worst mistakes in aviation,

364
00:14:22,358 --> 00:14:23,833
and it has happened.

365
00:14:24,013 --> 00:14:27,379
So the procedure is slow on purpose,

366
00:14:27,379 --> 00:14:29,342
even in an emergency.

367
00:14:29,522 --> 00:14:31,565
Confirm, then act.

368
00:14:31,745 --> 00:14:33,098
In the cockpit,

369
00:14:33,098 --> 00:14:36,796
speed is less important than being right.

370
00:14:37,145 --> 00:14:40,380
Fire handles are also checked before every

371
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:40,919
flight.

372
00:14:41,099 --> 00:14:43,337
Pilots press a test switch,

373
00:14:43,337 --> 00:14:46,405
and every fire warning light and bell

374
00:14:46,405 --> 00:14:47,483
must come on,

375
00:14:47,483 --> 00:14:50,468
to prove the detection system works.

376
00:14:50,648 --> 00:14:53,338
The test sounds alarming the first time

377
00:14:53,338 --> 00:14:55,200
you hear it from the cabin,

378
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:56,441
but it is routine.

379
00:14:56,621 --> 00:14:59,415
A warning system that cannot be tested

380
00:14:59,415 --> 00:15:02,136
is a warning system you cannot trust.

381
00:15:02,486 --> 00:15:05,093
Why is there a small microphone in

382
00:15:05,093 --> 00:15:06,626
the cockpit ceiling,

383
00:15:06,626 --> 00:15:08,082
between the pilots?

384
00:15:08,262 --> 00:15:10,803
It feeds the cockpit voice recorder,

385
00:15:10,803 --> 00:15:12,709
one of the two black boxes.

386
00:15:12,889 --> 00:15:15,339
It records the pilots' voices,

387
00:15:15,339 --> 00:15:17,953
the radio, and the sounds of the

388
00:15:17,953 --> 00:15:19,668
cockpit, like alarms,

389
00:15:19,668 --> 00:15:22,282
switches, and even engine noise.

390
00:15:22,462 --> 00:15:23,922
After an accident,

391
00:15:23,922 --> 00:15:27,493
those sounds can tell investigators what the

392
00:15:27,493 --> 00:15:29,522
crew knew, what they did,

393
00:15:29,522 --> 00:15:30,252
and when.

394
00:15:30,432 --> 00:15:31,990
And despite the name,

395
00:15:31,990 --> 00:15:34,959
black boxes are painted bright orange so

396
00:15:34,959 --> 00:15:36,295
they can be found.

397
00:15:36,644 --> 00:15:39,891
For years, cockpit voice recorders in the

398
00:15:39,891 --> 00:15:42,741
United States kept only the last two

399
00:15:42,741 --> 00:15:45,433
hours, recording over older audio.

400
00:15:45,613 --> 00:15:48,654
That meant important sounds could be erased

401
00:15:48,654 --> 00:15:51,200
if an airplane kept running after an

402
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:51,836
incident.

403
00:15:52,016 --> 00:15:53,840
In twenty twenty-six,

404
00:15:53,840 --> 00:15:58,445
the Federal Aviation Administration set a twenty-five

405
00:15:58,445 --> 00:16:01,920
hour standard for newly built airliners.

406
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:05,518
More recording means more chances to understand

407
00:16:05,518 --> 00:16:06,682
what went wrong,

408
00:16:06,682 --> 00:16:09,083
and to write the next rule before

409
00:16:09,083 --> 00:16:10,319
it happens again.

410
00:16:10,669 --> 00:16:13,107
The other black box is the flight

411
00:16:13,107 --> 00:16:14,141
data recorder.

412
00:16:14,321 --> 00:16:17,296
It stores thousands of measurements,

413
00:16:17,296 --> 00:16:19,031
like speed, altitude,

414
00:16:19,031 --> 00:16:22,172
control positions and engine settings,

415
00:16:22,172 --> 00:16:23,411
for many hours.

416
00:16:23,591 --> 00:16:26,679
Both boxes are built to survive extreme

417
00:16:26,679 --> 00:16:27,946
impact and fire,

418
00:16:27,946 --> 00:16:31,587
and they carry underwater locator beacons that

419
00:16:31,587 --> 00:16:34,358
ping for weeks if the airplane goes

420
00:16:34,358 --> 00:16:35,387
into the sea.

421
00:16:35,567 --> 00:16:39,352
Together, they turn a tragedy into lessons.

422
00:16:39,702 --> 00:16:41,107
So here are the ten.

423
00:16:41,287 --> 00:16:44,521
Different meals so both pilots never get

424
00:16:44,521 --> 00:16:45,653
sick together.

425
00:16:45,833 --> 00:16:48,491
A left seat for the final decision.

426
00:16:48,671 --> 00:16:51,504
Silence below ten thousand feet.

427
00:16:51,684 --> 00:16:54,093
A mask that goes on in five

428
00:16:54,093 --> 00:16:54,807
seconds.

429
00:16:54,987 --> 00:16:57,638
A rope for climbing out the window,

430
00:16:57,638 --> 00:16:59,759
and an axe for hidden fires.

431
00:16:59,939 --> 00:17:02,165
A heated windshield for birds,

432
00:17:02,165 --> 00:17:04,316
covers on dangerous switches,

433
00:17:04,316 --> 00:17:07,136
fire handles that are slow on purpose,

434
00:17:07,136 --> 00:17:09,881
and a microphone that listens for the

435
00:17:09,881 --> 00:17:10,771
next lesson.

436
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:14,211
The cockpit is where aviation keeps its

437
00:17:14,211 --> 00:17:14,766
memory.

438
00:17:14,946 --> 00:17:17,415
Almost every rule in it was written

439
00:17:17,415 --> 00:17:20,165
by an accident that should never happen

440
00:17:20,165 --> 00:17:20,588
again.

441
00:17:20,768 --> 00:17:23,847
If you want more machines explained part

442
00:17:23,847 --> 00:17:26,771
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

443
00:17:26,951 --> 00:17:28,964
And tell us in the comments which

444
00:17:28,964 --> 00:17:30,976
machine you want us to take apart

445
00:17:30,976 --> 00:17:31,281
next.
